/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: A cantilever truss consists of three joints: Wall supports A \(fixed) Tj
T*
(pin\) and B \(roller directly below A, vertical distance AB = 3.0 m\). Joint C is located) Tj
T*
(4.0 m to the right of joint B. A vertical downward load of P = 24 kN is applied at joint) Tj
T*
(C. Members are AC, BC, and vertical support line AB. Determine the forces in members AC) Tj
T*
(and BC.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Geometry of members at joint C:) Tj
T*
(In right triangle ABC: Horizontal base BC = 4.0 m, Vertical side AB = 3.0 m.) Tj
T*
(Inclined tie member AC length = sqrt\(4^2 + 3^2\) = 5.0 m.) Tj
T*
(Inclination angle theta of AC with horizontal BC: cos\(theta\) = 4/5 = 0.80, sin\(theta\) =) Tj
T*
(3/5 = 0.60.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Analyze Joint C using equilibrium:) Tj
T*
(Only two unknown member forces meet at joint C: F_AC \(pulling up and left at angle) Tj
T*
(theta\) and F_BC \(acting horizontally along BC\).) Tj
T*
(Assume F_AC is tensile \(pointing away from C\), F_BC is compressive \(pointing toward C,) Tj
T*
(or assume tensile to the left\):) Tj
T*
(Vertical Equilibrium \(Sigma Fy = 0 at C\):) Tj
T*
(+ F_AC * sin\(theta\) - P = 0 => F_AC * 0.60 = 24 kN => F_AC = 24 / 0.60 = + 40.0 kN) Tj
T*
(\(Tension\).) Tj
T*
(Horizontal Equilibrium \(Sigma Fx = 0 at C\):) Tj
T*
(- F_AC * cos\(theta\) - F_BC = 0 => F_BC = - F_AC * cos\(theta\) = - 40.0 * 0.80 = - 32.0 kN) Tj
T*
(\(Compression\).) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Verify Wall Support Reactions at A and B:) Tj
T*
(At A \(pin\): Member AC pulls joint A down and right with 40 kN => H_A = -32 kN \(left\),) Tj
T*
(V_A = +24 kN \(up\).) Tj
T*
(At B \(roller\): Member BC pushes joint B left with 32 kN => Normal reaction H_B = +32 kN) Tj
T*
(\(right\).) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Member AC = 40.0 kN \(Tension\), Member BC = 32.0 kN \(Compression\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
