(>> 4.7 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Differentiate comprehensively between the Method of Joints and the Method of) Tj
T*
(Sections, detailing when each is preferred.) Tj
T*
(Answer: The Method of Joints isolates individual joints as concurrent force systems and) Tj
T*
(solves unknown member forces sequentially using Sigma Fx = 0 and Sigma Fy = 0. It is) Tj
T*
(preferred when internal forces in ALL members of a truss must be cataloged for complete) Tj
T*
(structural fabrication. However, calculation errors at initial joints propagate to all) Tj
T*
(subsequent nodes. The Method of Sections passes a cutting plane severing not more than) Tj
T*
(three non-concurrent members, treating each isolated sub-structure as a rigid body and) Tj
T*
(applying Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma Fy = 0, and Sigma M = 0. It is highly preferred when only) Tj
T*
(specific internal chord or web members need verification, and errors do not propagate) Tj
T*
(since each member can be independently solved using moment equilibrium.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: What are zero-force members, and why are they incorporated into engineering) Tj
T*
(truss designs if they carry no force?) Tj
T*
(Answer: Zero-force members are structural elements in a truss that carry no axial) Tj
T*
(tensile or compressive force under a specific static loading condition. Despite carrying) Tj
T*
(zero force under normal primary loads, they are vital in real-world civil engineering) Tj
T*
(structures because: \(1\) They provide essential lateral bracing to prevent long slender) Tj
T*
(compression members from catastrophic elastic buckling. \(2\) They ensure stability and) Tj
T*
(prevent structural collapse when loading conditions change \(e.g. moving traffic loads,) Tj
T*
(wind gusts, seismic shaking\). \(3\) They maintain geometry during fabrication, lifting,) Tj
T*
(transport, and erection.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
